<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Gaya confederacy</h1><p class="st-meta">42 CE – 562 CE (approx.) · capital Gimhae, later Goryeong</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A league of iron-rich statelets in southern Korea that stood between Baekje and Silla for five centuries and traded closely with Japan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gaya developed from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a> statelets of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakdong-river">Nakdong</a> river. Tradition dates its founding to 42 CE, when six golden eggs descended from heaven at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a> and hatched the founders of six Gaya states, the eldest being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suro-of-geumgwan-gaya">Suro of Geumgwan Gaya</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Gaya never unified into a single kingdom. Its members, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/geumgwan-gaya">Geumgwan Gaya</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ara-gaya">Ara Gaya</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/daegaya">Daegaya</a>, were led first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a> and, after a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> expedition of 400 broke its power, by Daegaya at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goryeong">Goryeong</a>. Gaya exported iron ingots and armour, made fine stoneware, and kept close ties with Wa; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon shoki</a> calls part of it Mimana. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-gasil">King Gasil</a> of Daegaya is credited with devising the gayageum zither, for which the musician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ureuk">Ureuk</a> composed music.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a> absorbed the states one by one: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/geumgwan-gaya">Geumgwan Gaya</a> surrendered in 532 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/daegaya">Daegaya</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinheung-of-silla">King Jinheung</a> in 562. Gaya's elite and craftsmen were integrated into Silla, notably the family of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gaya-confederacy.jpg" alt="Gaya confederacy" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:5th_century_Kaya_confederacy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>42 CE – 562 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gimhae, later Goryeong</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Suro of Geumgwan Gaya, Guhyeong of Geumgwan Gaya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs, later Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaya language</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gaya, Kaya, Garak, Mimana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=302&amp;focus=gaya-confederacy" aria-label="Gaya confederacy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7927 906 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gaya confederacy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8247 1162L8260 1139M8247 1162L8263 1160M8247 1162L8236 1137M8247 1162L8247 1162"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8260 1139h0M8263 1160h0M8236 1137h0M8247 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8247 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8247 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=302&amp;focus=gaya-confederacy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
